// A closure with no captures (Ch. 4 §4.1).
//
// It is three things at once and the shape is worth reading whole: a
// **type**, whose fields are the addresses of what it captured — none here,
// so a type with no fields, which is still a tryte because storage always
// has an address; a **function**, `<name>.call`, taking that type by
// reference and then what was written; and a **type argument**, because
// `impl Fn(A) -> R` in argument position is a type parameter nobody wrote
// (§2.2), so `apply` is one function per closure passed to it.
//
// The name is `main.closure1` — which closure of which function, and
// nothing about how either was numbered (G9.67).
//
// A closure that *captures* is not here: reading a capture is a dereference
// of a reference value, and that is the one expression this lowering has
// never had. Such a closure is refused, and because a refused `.call` would
// leave `main` calling a name nothing defines, the whole module is refused
// with it.

fn apply(g: impl Fn(t27) -> t27, x: t27) -> t27 { g(x) }

fn twice(g: impl Fn(t27) -> t27, x: t27) -> t27 { apply(g, x) }

fn main() -> t27 {
    apply(|x: t27| x + 1, 2)
}
